---
title: Postboi compared
name: Postboi compared
description: How Postboi stacks up against Resend, SendGrid, Postmark, Mailtrap and nodemailer. Postboi is an email provider in its own right, and using someone else's is optional.
category: Getting started
---

If you're choosing between Postboi and another email service, start here: **Postboi is an
email provider.** We run the sending infrastructure, same as Resend, Postmark or SendGrid do,
and `postboi` on npm is the SDK for it. A `POSTBOI_TOKEN` is all you need. You don't need an
account anywhere else.

On top of that, the same `mail()` call can send through another provider if you'd like it
to, and the same API sends SMS, WhatsApp, push and chat as well. Both are extras.

## Is Postboi a wrapper around other providers?

No. We support 40-odd other providers, which makes it easy to assume Postboi sits on top of
them. It works the other way round:

1. **Postboi is an email service.** Its mail is delivered by Amazon SES in the EU, each team
   as its own SES tenant, and [the Postboi provider](/provider) handles sending, custom
   domains, lists and broadcasts, contacts, double opt-in, suppressions, a message log,
   delivery webhooks, scheduling, hosted forms, receiving, email testing and a dashboard. It
   also runs [Tempboi](/temp-inbox), throwaway inboxes at tempboi.email for tests and agents.
2. **`postboi` is its SDK.** `mail()` and friends, with one error type, in any JS framework.
3. **You can point the SDK elsewhere.** Send through [another provider](/providers) instead,
   or keep our log and dashboard while [relaying](/provider#relay-send-via-your-own-provider)
   delivery through your own Resend, Postmark or SES account.

So "Postboi or Resend?" compares two email providers.

## Is Postboi built for transactional email?

Yes, it's the main thing it's for. Sign-in links, one-time codes, receipts and password
resets are what the provider is built around:

- **[Delivery status](/provider#delivery-status)** on every message, with bounces and
  complaints tracked and the addresses suppressed for you.
- **[Idempotency keys](/provider#batching--idempotency)**, so retrying a send after a
  timeout can't send it twice, and [retries](/errors#retries) with backoff built into the SDK.
- **[Webhooks](/webhooks)** for every delivery event, and message lookup from code.
- **Sending pauses itself** if bounce or complaint rates climb past a safety threshold,
  which protects your domain's reputation before a mailbox provider has to.
- **[Receiving](/provider#replies)**, so a reply to a transactional email isn't lost.

The other channels and the provider switch are extras on top of that.

## Postboi vs Resend

|                                                  | Postboi                                        | Resend                         |
| ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Email provider with managed sending              | ✅                                             | ✅                             |
| TypeScript SDK                                   | ✅ `mail()`                                    | ✅ `resend.emails.send()`      |
| Works without any other provider account         | ✅                                             | ✅                             |
| First send with no DNS and no card               | ✅ from `you@send.postboi.email`               | Needs a verified domain for real recipients |
| Custom domains, webhooks, scheduling             | ✅                                             | ✅                             |
| Idempotency keys, suppressions, bounce tracking  | ✅                                             | ✅                             |
| Receiving email                                  | ✅                                             | ✅                             |
| Lists, broadcasts, contacts                      | ✅                                             | ✅                             |
| Switch provider without rewriting your code      | ✅ one line of config                          | ❌ your code calls Resend's API |
| SMS, WhatsApp, push and chat in the same API     | ✅                                             | ❌ email only                  |
| Turn `FormData` into an HTML email               | ✅                                             | ❌                             |
| Hosted form endpoints (no backend)               | ✅                                             | ❌                             |
| Local dev inbox, no code changes                 | ✅ `/__postboi`                                | ❌                             |
| Throwaway inboxes for tests and agents           | ✅ [Tempboi](/temp-inbox), no account          | Test addresses that simulate delivery and bounces |
| An agent can set it up without you signing in    | ✅ `postboi init --agent`                      | Needs an account and API key first |
| Free tier                                        | 3,000 a month, 100 a day                       | 3,000 a month, 100 a day       |
| First paid plan                                  | £9 for 40,000 a month                          | $20 for 50,000 a month         |
| 50,000 emails a month                            | £13 (about $17)                                | $20                            |
| 100,000 emails a month                           | £25 (about $33)                                | $35                            |
| Lists and broadcasts                             | Included in every plan                         | A separate plan, priced by contact |
| Ecosystem and track record                       | Newer, with a [measured delivery record](https://postboi.app/delivery) | ✅ bigger, been around longer  |

This is Resend as we understand it in September 2026, with dollars at 1.32 to the pound. Check
[resend.com/pricing](https://resend.com/pricing) for what it charges today, and
[Postboi or Resend?](https://postboi.app/compare/resend) for the cost at every volume.

**Pick Postboi** if you want to pay less for the same volume, as little code as possible
between your app and a sent email, if an AI agent is setting things up, if you'll want SMS or
push later, or if you'd like to be able to change provider without touching your code.

**Pick Resend** if you want its ecosystem, its React Email tooling (React Email works with
Postboi too, since `body` takes any HTML) or its longer history in production.

Already using Resend? You can keep it. [`postboi/resend`](/providers) sends through your
Resend account using Postboi's API, and if you move to the Postboi provider later, you change
`provider: "resend"` to `provider: "postboi"` and that's it.

## Postboi vs SendGrid, Postmark, Mailgun or SES

Same story as Resend. Each is an email provider with its own SDK, and code you write against
that SDK only sends through them. Postboi is also an email provider, and its SDK can send
through any of them too. So you can use Postboi instead of them, or keep one and stop your
app depending on it directly.

## Postboi vs Mailtrap, Mailosaur and temp mail APIs

These are testing services: a sandbox that catches what your app sends in staging, or
inboxes a test can receive at. Postboi has both halves, beside the sending:

- **[Dev inbox](/dev-inbox)** catches everything your app sends in development, locally,
  with no account and no code changes.
- **[Tempboi](/temp-inbox)** gives a test or an agent a real address at tempboi.email that
  receives mail from anywhere, with the code and link already pulled out. No account.
- **[Email testing](/email-testing)** checks an email for client support, clipping,
  accessibility and deliverability before it's sent.

The difference is that the same SDK then sends your production mail, so the email your
tests read is the one your users get. If you already use Mailtrap for sending, `mail()` can
send through it too (`postboi/mailtrap`).

## Postboi vs nodemailer

nodemailer is a library. You still need an SMTP server or a provider behind it, and you
don't get a delivery log, suppressions or webhooks. Postboi is a provider and a library, so
`bunx postboi init` replaces installing nodemailer, signing up somewhere and pasting in SMTP
credentials. If you do want SMTP, Postboi supports that too (`postboi/smtp`).

## Why it works well with AI agents

- **Setup is one command and nobody has to sign in.** `bunx postboi init --agent` creates a
  project the agent can use straight away. You claim it with one click when you're ready.
- **An agent can receive mail too.** [Tempboi](/temp-inbox) gives it an address with no
  account, for signing up to things and reading the code. [Email for agents](/agents) has
  the whole picture.
- **Every channel looks the same.** `mail({ to, subject, body })` writes the plain-text
  version for you, and `sms()`, `push()` and the rest take the same shape and fail the same
  way.
- **Errors have one shape.** Whichever provider fails, you get a `PostboiError`, with a
  `code` when the provider gave one. On the Postboi provider that's one of ours, like
  `rate_limited` or `from_not_allowed`. Other providers pass their own codes through.
- **Mistakes show up as type errors.** `postboi sync` types `from` to the addresses your
  account can send from, and form names to the forms you have.
- **The docs are easy for a model to read.** The package ships a skill
  (`bunx postboi skill`), every page is plain Markdown at `/raw/<slug>`, and the whole site
  is one file at [`/llms-full.txt`](https://docs.postboi.app/llms-full.txt).
